Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant columns and their usage  
• `year`: filter data for 2008 (to find the 5 most‑frequent minor categories) and to build annual summaries for every other year.  
• `minor_category`: identify the crime type; group by it to find the “top‑5” list and to compute yearly subtotals.  
• `value`: numeric count of crimes; aggregate with SUM(value) to get totals.  
• (optional) `borough`, `lsoa_code`, `major_category`, `month`: no direct need for the requested London‑wide shares, but can be kept for extra slicing or checks.

Typical conditions that will be used  
• `WHERE year = 2008` – to isolate the base year that defines the top 5 categories.  
• `WHERE minor_category IN ( …top‑5 list… )` – to keep only the five selected categories when computing yearly shares.  

Below are ten BigQuery SELECT statements – moving from the simplest data inspections to the final query that returns the annual percentage shares (rounded to two decimals) for the 5 leading minor crime categories of 2008.  
Every query is limited to 20 rows so you can run them safely.

```sql
-- 1) Description: Quick look at raw rows to understand the table structure.
SELECT *
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Description: Inspect distinct years that exist in the dataset.
SELECT DISTINCT year
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
ORDER BY year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Description: List the distinct minor crime categories that occur in 2008.
SELECT DISTINCT minor_category
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE year = 2008
ORDER BY minor_category
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Description: Total number of crimes recorded in London for each year.
SELECT year, SUM(value) AS total_crimes
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
GROUP BY year
ORDER BY year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Description: Crime counts per minor category for 2008, highest first.
SELECT minor_category, SUM(value) AS crimes_in_2008
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE year = 2008
GROUP BY minor_category
ORDER BY crimes_in_2008 DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Description: Grab exactly the five most common 2008 minor categories.
-- (This is the subquery that will feed later steps.)
SELECT minor_category
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
WHERE year = 2008
GROUP BY minor_category
ORDER BY SUM(value) DESC
LIMIT 5;
```

```sql
-- 7) Description: Yearly totals for each of those 5 categories only.
WITH top_5 AS (
  SELECT minor_category
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
  WHERE year = 2008
  GROUP BY minor_category
  ORDER BY SUM(value) DESC
  LIMIT 5
)
SELECT c.year,
       c.minor_category,
       SUM(c.value) AS crimes_for_category
FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa` AS c
JOIN top_5 AS t
ON  c.minor_category = t.minor_category
GROUP BY c.year, c.minor_category
ORDER BY c.year, crimes_for_category DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Description: Combine yearly totals for *all* crimes with the
--    totals for the selected 5 categories (so shares can be calculated).
WITH year_totals AS (
  SELECT year, SUM(value) AS total_crimes
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
  GROUP BY year
), top_5 AS (
  SELECT minor_category
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
  WHERE year = 2008
  GROUP BY minor_category
  ORDER BY SUM(value) DESC
  LIMIT 5
), cat_year AS (
  SELECT c.year, c.minor_category, SUM(c.value) AS cat_crimes
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa` c
  JOIN top_5 t
  ON  c.minor_category = t.minor_category
  GROUP BY c.year, c.minor_category
)
SELECT y.year,
       cy.minor_category,
       ROUND(100 * cy.cat_crimes / y.total_crimes, 2) AS pct_share
FROM year_totals AS y
JOIN cat_year    AS cy
ON  y.year = cy.year
ORDER BY y.year, pct_share DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) Description: Same as #8 but aggregate the five category shares into
--    a single ARRAY per year (one row per year as requested, JSON‑like view).
WITH year_totals AS (
  SELECT year, SUM(value) AS total_crimes
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
  GROUP BY year
), top_5 AS (
  SELECT minor_category
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
  WHERE year = 2008
  GROUP BY minor_category
  ORDER BY SUM(value) DESC
  LIMIT 5
), cat_year AS (
  SELECT c.year, c.minor_category, SUM(c.value) AS cat_crimes
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa` c
  JOIN top_5 t
  ON  c.minor_category = t.minor_category
  GROUP BY c.year, c.minor_category
)
SELECT y.year,
       ARRAY_AGG(STRUCT(cy.minor_category,
                        ROUND(100 * cy.cat_crimes / y.total_crimes, 2) AS pct_share)
                 ORDER BY pct_share DESC) AS category_shares
FROM year_totals AS y
JOIN cat_year    AS cy
ON  y.year = cy.year
GROUP BY y.year
ORDER BY y.year
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10) Description: Final answer – one row per year, five columns with
--      the percentage shares of each top‑5 category (2008 definition),
--      rounded to 2 decimals.
WITH top_5 AS (
  SELECT minor_category
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
  WHERE year = 2008
  GROUP BY minor_category
  ORDER BY SUM(value) DESC
  LIMIT 5
),
year_totals AS (
  SELECT year, SUM(value) AS total_crimes
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa`
  GROUP BY year
),
cat_year AS (
  SELECT c.year, c.minor_category, SUM(c.value) AS cat_crimes
  FROM `bigquery-public-data.london_crime.crime_by_lsoa` AS c
  JOIN top_5 AS t
  ON  c.minor_category = t.minor_category
  GROUP BY c.year, c.minor_category
),
shares AS (
  SELECT
    y.year,
    cy.minor_category,
    ROUND(100 * cy.cat_crimes / y.total_crimes, 2) AS pct_share
  FROM year_totals AS y
  JOIN cat_year    AS cy
  ON  y.year = cy.year
)
SELECT *
FROM shares
PIVOT (
  MAX(pct_share) FOR minor_category IN (
    -- the five category names are selected dynamically by the top_5 CTE
    SELECT minor_category FROM top_5
  )
)
ORDER BY year
LIMIT 20;
```

The final query (#10) returns one row per year and five “percentage share” columns—one for each minor crime category that ranked in the top‑5 during 2008—so you can clearly see how the contribution of those categories evolves over time.
